# Pantheon Harness — Caddy reverse proxy (Bible §11; LAN/Tailscale only, NO public ingress)
#
# Two named sites on the LAN. `tls internal` mints a local CA cert — on first browser
# visit, trust Caddy's root CA (deploy/README.md §TLS) so HTTPS is green on the LAN.
# Peta is deliberately NOT proxied here (bound to 127.0.0.1 in compose — TM-007).
# The Facade's /v1/chat/completions is NOT proxied either: only LibreChat (in-network)
# and same-host services call it. Nothing here is reachable from the public internet
# (pfSense/no port-forward + Tailscale is the outer wall; verify with the M2 test).

# ---- global options -----------------------------------------------------------------
# default_sni: the household edge proxy (Caddy CT 1052 on 10.100.23.52) reaches this host by
# IP, so its TLS ClientHello carries NO SNI and the handshake fails with no default set.
# Naming the household hostname keeps that hop ENCRYPTED — the alternative was a plaintext
# HTTP hop — and lets the service-intake platform emit its stock `https-self-signed` block.
{
	default_sni pantheon.ferrumcorde.com
}

# ---- shared security-header snippet (Bible §11 header list) -------------------------
(security_headers) {
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"
		-Server
	}
}

# ---- LibreChat (chat UI) -------------------------------------------------------------
# Adjust the hostname to your LAN DNS (pfSense host override or /etc/hosts entries).
# `pantheon.ferrumcorde.com` is the household name, published by the service-intake platform
# (10.100.23.60) into the household Caddy + both Pi-holes. INTERNAL DNS ONLY — no public record,
# no tunnel; same posture as alden.ferrumcorde.com (ruling 2026-08-18).
# The bare VM IP is a third site address ON PURPOSE: the household edge proxy dials this host
# as `https://192.168.1.93:443` and its request arrives with the IP as Host/:authority, which
# matches no named site — and Caddy answers an unmatched site with an EMPTY 200 (silent, and
# not written to this site's access log). Listing the IP routes that request to the chat UI
# like any other. Verified 2026-08-19: without it the household route serves a zero-byte 200.
pantheon.lan, pantheon.ferrumcorde.com, 192.168.1.93 {
	log
	tls internal
	import security_headers
	header X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"
	# The console UNDER the chat address (ruling 2026-08-27, docs/2026-08-27-harness-under-chat-
	# address-design.md): /harness/* goes to the admin service with the prefix stripped and announced
	# in X-Forwarded-Prefix — the service builds every link, redirect, asset and WebSocket URL from
	# that header. LibreChat's own /login, /api/* and /assets/* are untouched. SAMEORIGIN above is
	# what lets the harness embed the chat page as a tab. The admin site below stays a root mount.
	redir /harness /harness/ 308
	handle_path /harness/* {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8088 {
			header_up X-Forwarded-Prefix /harness
		}
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy librechat:3080
	}
}

# ---- Control-plane admin service (config page, approvals, harness frame, terminals) --
# `192.168.1.93:8443` is the household entrance to this console (published as
# pantheon-admin.ferrumcorde.com by the service-intake platform). It is a separate PORT, not a
# second hostname, on purpose: the household edge proxy rewrites Host to the backend address
# (verified 2026-08-19 — `Host: 192.168.1.93:443`, real name only in X-Forwarded-Host), so two
# household names cannot be told apart on one port. Port 8443 disambiguates without trusting a
# forgeable header. Publish it in docker-compose.yml alongside 80/443.
admin.pantheon.lan, 192.168.1.93:8443 {
	tls internal
	import security_headers
	header X-Frame-Options "DENY"
	# WebSocket upgrade for /terminal/* works through reverse_proxy automatically.
	# Root mount: a client-supplied X-Forwarded-Prefix must never reach the service here
	# (audit 2026-08-27 F4) — only the chat site above sets it, and there it overwrites.
	reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8088 {
		header_up -X-Forwarded-Prefix
	}
}
